<https://workflowhub.eu/presentations/4?version=1> a ns1:PresentationDigitalDocument ;
    dcterms:conformsTo "https://schema.org/PresentationDigitalDocument" ;
    ns1:creator <https://workflowhub.eu/people/2> ;
    ns1:dateCreated "2021-07-05T12:10:35Z"^^ns1:Date ;
    ns1:dateModified "2021-07-05T12:12:32Z"^^ns1:Date ;
    ns1:description """BioCompute Object (BCO) is a standard (IEEE 2791-2020) for describing computational workflows for regularory submission, e.g. a genomics workflow as part of personalized medicine.\r
\r
RO-Crate is a community-based specification for research data packaging of Research Objects with rich metadata, based on open standards and vocabularies like JSON-LD and schema.org.\r
\r
BCO RO-Crate is the combination of these two approaches, packaging a BCO and the workflow it describes in a RO-Crate.\r
\r
This webinar describes the tutorial on how to make the exemplar BCO RO-Crate, starting with a workflow run.\r
\r
The tutorial is based on an exemplar run of the Nextflow workflow nf-core/chipseq version 1.2.1 using test inputs, capture the results in a BCO which we then describe further as an RO-Crate, and finally package all the files using BagIt.\r
\r
* Slides: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4633732\r
* [BCO tutorial](https://workflowhub.eu/sops/6)""" ;
    ns1:encodingFormat "text/html" ;
    ns1:keywords "ro-crate, bco" ;
    ns1:license <https://spdx.org/licenses/CC-BY-4.0> ;
    ns1:name "Describing and packaging workflows using RO-Crate and BioCompute Objects" ;
    ns1:producer <https://workflowhub.eu/projects/45> ;
    ns1:url <https://workflowhub.eu/presentations/4?version=1> ;
    ns1:version 1 .

